Helios DXVK fork: discard slices recycle the most recently freed allocations first, lock-free

Conduit guest/windows. Applied after 0021 by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1.

A freed cacheable allocation (a dynamic buffer's discard slice from a local
allocation cache, 0021) went into its size class's free list under a mutex,
and reached a local cache again only once that free list had filled to a
whole list (256 KiB worth: 1024 slices of 256 B) and moved to the pool. A
refill therefore handed out slices freed up to a list's worth of frees ago,
likely cold in the CPU caches, where the allocator's own path hands back the
address it freed last.

Each size class of the shared cache now also keeps a lock-free stack of the
most recently freed allocations, newest first. A free pushes onto it (CAS);
a local cache refill takes the whole stack (one exchange) before it looks at
the full lists. There is no single-element pop, so there is no ABA. A stack
holds at most 16 lists' worth (4 MiB); further frees take the free-list path
as before. A stack nobody takes for a second goes back to the allocator at
the periodic cleanup, like drained lists.

